===Etymology 2===
From {{der|en|enm|ringen}}, from {{der|en|ang|hringan|hrinġan|to ring}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*hringijaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|ringen}}, {{cog|sv|ringa}}. Of [[imitative]] origin.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The resonant sound of a [[bell]], or a sound resembling it.
#: {{ux|en|The church bell's '''ring''' could be heard the length of the valley.}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''ring''' of hammer on anvil filled the air.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[pleasant]] or [[correct]] [[sound]].
#: {{ux|en|The name has a nice '''ring''' to it.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A sound or appearance that is characteristic of something.
#: {{ux|en|Her statements in court had a '''ring''' of falsehood.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A telephone call.
#: {{ux|en|I’ll give you a '''ring''' when the plane lands.}}
# Any loud sound; the sound of numerous voices; a sound continued, repeated, or reverberated.
#* {{RQ:Bacon Henry 7
|passage=the '''ring''' of acclamations fresh in his ears}}
# A [[chime]], or set of bells harmonically tuned.
#: {{ux|en|St Mary's has a '''ring''' of eight bells.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Church History
|passage=as great and tunable a '''ring''' of bells as any in the world}}

